AB  - This paper discusses the body size/body mass-population density relationship as well as the biomass-body size distribution of nematodes from Lake Königssee and Lake Brunnsee in Germany. Major points of Strayer [See Journal of the North American Benthological Society (1991) 10, 210-221] are validated, i.e. (1) the size structures of marine and freshwater zoobenthic communities differ greatly; (2) this difference may have an evolutionary origin because animals with life history traits of Warwick's [See Oecologia (1984) 61, 32-41] marine macrofaunal groups are scarce in freshwaters; and (3) lakes may differ in their zoobenthic size structures in response, at least, to differences in water chemistry.
